# Network+ VLAN Segmentation & Inter-VLAN Routing Lab

## Project Overview

This project demonstrates the design and configuration of a segmented network using Cisco VLANs and inter-VLAN routing in Cisco Packet Tracer.

The network separates three departments—HR, IT, and Operations—into individual VLANs and IP networks. Router-on-a-stick and 802.1Q trunking are then used to provide communication between the VLANs.

## Network Design

| VLAN | Department | Network | Default Gateway |
|------|------------|---------|-----------------|
| 10 | HR | 192.168.10.0/24 | 192.168.10.1 |
| 20 | IT | 192.168.20.0/24 | 192.168.20.1 |
| 30 | Operations | 192.168.30.0/24 | 192.168.30.1 |
## Implementation

### VLAN Configuration

Three VLANs were created to logically separate the departments:

- VLAN 10 — HR
- VLAN 20 — IT
- VLAN 30 — Operations

Access ports were assigned as follows:

| Switch Ports | VLAN | Department |
|---|---|---|
| Fa0/1 - Fa0/3 | 10 | HR |
| Fa0/4 - Fa0/5 | 20 | IT |
| Fa0/6 - Fa0/7 | 30 | Operations |
| Fa0/8 | Trunk | Router Connection |

### 802.1Q Trunk

FastEthernet0/8 was configured as a trunk to carry VLAN 10, 20, and 30 traffic between the switch and router.

### Router-on-a-Stick

The router uses three subinterfaces:

| Router Interface | VLAN | Gateway |
|---|---|---|
| G0/0.10 | 10 | 192.168.10.1 |
| G0/0.20 | 20 | 192.168.20.1 |
| G0/0.30 | 30 | 192.168.30.1 |
## Testing and Verification

Connectivity testing was performed in stages to verify both VLAN segmentation and inter-VLAN routing.

### Test 1 — Same-VLAN Communication

Devices within the same VLAN were able to successfully ping each other.

Examples:

- VLAN 10 → VLAN 10: Successful
- VLAN 20 → VLAN 20: Successful
- VLAN 30 → VLAN 30: Successful

This verified that the access-port assignments and VLAN configurations were functioning correctly.

### Test 2 — VLAN Isolation

Before inter-VLAN routing was configured, devices in different VLANs could not communicate.

Example:

VLAN 10 → VLAN 20: Failed

This confirmed that the VLANs were operating as separate Layer 2 broadcast domains.

### Test 3 — Inter-VLAN Routing

Router-on-a-stick was configured using 802.1Q subinterfaces and a default gateway for each VLAN.

After routing was configured:

- VLAN 10 → VLAN 20: Successful
- VLAN 10 → VLAN 30: Successful
- VLAN 20 → VLAN 30: Successful

This verified successful Layer 3 communication between all three VLANs.

## Troubleshooting

During testing, a workstation in VLAN 10 could communicate with devices within its own VLAN but could not reach devices in other VLANs.

I tested connectivity from the workstation to its local default gateway and discovered that the workstation did not have a default gateway configured.

The default gateway `192.168.10.1` was added to the workstation. After correcting the configuration, inter-VLAN communication was successfully verified.

This demonstrated a structured troubleshooting approach:

1. Verify local IP configuration.
2. Test same-VLAN connectivity.
3. Test connectivity to the default gateway.
4. Test connectivity to remote VLANs.
5. Verify routing and trunk configuration if necessary.

## Skills Demonstrated

- VLAN creation and segmentation
- Cisco IOS configuration
- Access-port configuration
- 802.1Q trunking
- Router-on-a-stick
- Router subinterfaces
- IPv4 addressing and subnetting
- Default gateway configuration
- Inter-VLAN routing
- Network connectivity testing
- Layer 2 and Layer 3 troubleshooting
